import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// DRAFT COPY — structure and constraints are settled; the wording is not.
// Everything below is grounded in CONTEXT.md, the positioning decision, and
// the Clients named in src/content/projects/. Nothing here is invented
// biography, because there are no notes for this page yet. Felicia rewrites
// it in her own voice; the tests guard the constraints, not the sentences.
//
// What the 2016 page did that this one must not (issue #7): a three-question
// Q&A, a published $75/hour rate, and an answer stating the site was built
// with Pico — false the moment it was migrated.
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About · Firefly Web Services"
	description="Felicia Betancourt — a solo web engineer working with nonprofits, unions, research labs, arts groups, and civic projects."
>
	<h1>Hello — I'm Felicia.</h1>

	<p>
		I'm a web engineer, and Firefly Web Services is the name I practice under. It's a shingle
		over a one-person shop, not an agency: when you hire Firefly, I am who you get, start to
		finish.
	</p>

	<section aria-labelledby="who-heading">
		<h2 id="who-heading">Who I work with</h2>
		<p>
			Mission-Driven Organizations, mostly — nonprofits, unions, research labs, arts groups,
			and civic projects. An animal shelter whose adoption listings had to stay accurate
			without doubling anyone's data entry. An electrical workers' union that needed a site
			its members could read on a phone. A sacred arts organization selling work online. And,
			as a volunteer since 2015, a city budget made legible to the people it belongs to.
		</p>
		<p>
			That's a deliberate choice rather than an accident of who called. These organizations
			tend to have real constraints — a budget that has to be justified to a board, a staff
			with no one whose job is the website, a timeline set by a grant. I'd rather do careful
			work inside those constraints than pretend they don't exist.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="how-heading">
		<h2 id="how-heading">How I work</h2>
		<p>
			A good deal of what I build is WordPress, because that is where these organizations
			already are, and moving a working site off a platform the staff understands is rarely
			the favor it appears to be. When something else fits better — a static build, a small
			service, a data pipeline between two systems that were never meant to talk — I'll say
			so, and say why.
		</p>
		<p>
			I try to leave things better documented than I found them, and to hand back something
			maintainable by whoever comes next, including the version of you who has forgotten why
			any of it works that way. Accessibility and performance aren't a separate line item
			here; a site that's slow on a phone or unusable with a keyboard isn't finished.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		If any of that sounds like what you need, <a href="/hire/">tell me about your project</a>.
	</p>
</BaseLayout>
